module

public import WeilConjectures.Mathlib.RingTheory.Henselian
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Valuation.ValuationRing
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.Away.Basic
public import Mathlib.Topology.Algebra.Valued.ValuationTopology
import WeilConjectures.RingTheory.IntegralOverHenselianPair
import WeilConjectures.RingTheory.HenselianLocalRootLifting

/-!
# Valuation quotient of an admissibly principal henselian local ring

Source: Illusie--Laszlo--Orgogozo, Travaux de Gabber, XX 4.4, printed pp. 373--374,
the paragraph beginning "Say I = (phi)".
(blueprint: lem-per-admissible-modification-valuative-ring-reduction)
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

namespace RingTheory

/-- Suppose every finitely generated ideal containing a positive power of a regular
nonunit `φ` is generated by a nonzerodivisor. The intersection `P` of those powers
is prime; inverting `φ` gives a henselian local ring with maximal ideal `PS`, and
`B / P` is a henselian valuation ring. The canonical quotient map identifies
`S / PS` with `(B / P)[1 / φ]` and then with the fraction field of `B / P`.
The positive powers of the image of `φ` form a zero-neighbourhood basis for the
intrinsic valuation topology on `B / P`.

Source: Illusie--Laszlo--Orgogozo, Travaux de Gabber, XX 4.4, printed pp. 373--374.
(blueprint: lem-per-admissible-modification-valuative-ring-reduction)

Proof:
1. For `a ∉ P`, choose `n ≥ 1` with `a ∉ φⁿB`. The admissible ideal `(a, φⁿ)`
   has a regular generator `d`. Write `a = du`, `φⁿ = dv` and `d = ra + sφⁿ`.
   Cancel `d` to obtain `1 = ru + sv`. Locality implies that `u` or `v` is a
   unit. The latter contradicts the choice of `n`; hence `a` is regular and
   divides a positive power of `φ`.
2. No power of `φ`, including the zeroth, lies in `P`: regularity would make
   `φ` a unit. If `a,b ∉ P`, choose powers divisible by `a,b` using step 1.
   Their product excludes `ab ∈ P`. Thus `P` is proper prime. The quotient
   `V = B / P` is a local domain, and the image of `φ` is nonzero and a nonunit.
3. For `a ∉ P`, choose `φʳ = ae`. If `p ∈ P`, write `p = φʳb = a(eb)`.
   For every `n`, also write `p = φʳ⁺ⁿbₙ`; regularity of `a` implies
   `eb = eφⁿbₙ ∈ φⁿB`. Thus `P = aP`; in particular `P = φP = d²P` for `d ∉ P`.
4. Regularity embeds `B` in `S = B[1 / φ]`. Step 3 gives `PS = P` as subsets
   of `S`. A fraction with numerator outside `P` is a unit by step 1, whereas
   a fraction with numerator in `P` cannot be a unit by step 2. Hence `S` is
   local, its maximal ideal is `PS`, and `PS ∩ B = P`.
5. For nonzero elements of `V`, lift them to `a,b ∉ P`. The ideal `(a,b)` is
   admissible by step 1. Factor through its regular generator and cancel as
   in step 1. One of the two remaining factors is a unit, so `a ∣ b` or `b ∣ a`.
   Reduce modulo `P` to get `ValuationRing V` (def-trf-valuation-ring).
6. The canonical surjection `S → V[1 / φ]` has kernel `PS`, since `V` is a
   domain and the image of `φ` is nonzero. Each nonzero element of `V` divides
   a power of that image by step 1. Thus `V[1 / φ]` is the fraction field of
   `V`. The two ring equivalences have the displayed canonical compatibilities.
7. Apply lem-weil-pbc-integral-over-henselian-pair to the integral quotient
   `B → V` and the maximal-ideal henselian pair of `B`. Its extended ideal is
   the maximal ideal of `V`, proving henselianity of `V`.
8. Given a monic `F ∈ S[X]` and a simple residue root, lift it to `a = b / φᵏ`.
   Clear denominators in `G(X) = φˡ F(X / φᵏ)`, with `l ≥ k`. Then `G(b) ∈ P`
   and `d = G'(b)` is a unit of `S`, hence lies outside `P`. Step 3 gives
   `G(b) = d²c` for some `c ∈ P`.
9. Write `G(b+Y) = d²c + dY + ∑ gᵢYⁱ` with `2 ≤ i ≤ N`, and put
   `hᵢ = gᵢdⁱ⁻²`. The monic polynomial
   `Q(Z) = Zᴺ + Zᴺ⁻¹ + ∑ hᵢcⁱ⁻¹Zᴺ⁻ⁱ` reduces to `Zᴺ⁻¹(Z+1)`.
   Its residue root `-1` is simple. By lem-weil-pbc-henselian-local-root-lifting,
   lift it to a unit root `z`. Put `t = c/z ∈ P`. Substitution gives
   `c+t+∑hᵢtⁱ = cz⁻ᴺQ(z) = 0`, hence `G(b+dt)=0`. This gives a root of `F`
   congruent to `a` modulo `PS`. The reverse monic-root criterion of that
   same lemma proves henselianity of `S`.
10. For the additive valuation of `V`, put `δ = v(φ) > 0`. Every nonzero
    element of `V` divides a power of `φ` by step 1. Consequently the positive
    multiples of `δ` are cofinal in the value group. Each ideal `φⁿV` contains
    the strict ball of radius `nδ`, and each strict ball contains such an ideal
    by cofinality. This proves the asserted basis for the valuation topology.
    Mathlib uses the equivalent multiplicative valuation, so its strict balls
    are written with `<` rather than additive `>`. -/
theorem admissibleModification_valuative_ring_reduction
    (B : Type u) [CommRing B] [HenselianLocalRing B]
    (φ : B) (hNonunit : ¬ IsUnit φ) (hRegular : φ ∈ nonZeroDivisors B)
    (hPrincipal : ∀ J : Ideal B, J.FG →
      (∃ n : ℕ, 1 ≤ n ∧ Ideal.span {φ ^ n} ≤ J) →
        ∃ d : B, d ∈ nonZeroDivisors B ∧ J = Ideal.span {d})
    (P : Ideal B) (hP : P = ⨅ n : ℕ, Ideal.span {φ ^ (n + 1)}) :
    ∃ (_ : P.IsPrime) (_ : HenselianLocalRing (Localization.Away φ))
      (_ : HenselianLocalRing (B ⧸ P)) (_ : ValuationRing (B ⧸ P)),
      IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal (Localization.Away φ) =
        P.map (algebraMap B (Localization.Away φ)) ∧
      Ideal.Quotient.mk P φ ≠ 0 ∧ ¬ IsUnit (Ideal.Quotient.mk P φ) ∧
      (∃ e : (Localization.Away φ ⧸ P.map (algebraMap B (Localization.Away φ))) ≃+*
          Localization.Away (Ideal.Quotient.mk P φ),
        ∀ s : Localization.Away φ,
          e (Ideal.Quotient.mk (P.map (algebraMap B (Localization.Away φ))) s) =
            IsLocalization.Away.map (Localization.Away φ)
              (Localization.Away (Ideal.Quotient.mk P φ)) (Ideal.Quotient.mk P) φ s) ∧
      (∃ e : Localization.Away (Ideal.Quotient.mk P φ) ≃+* FractionRing (B ⧸ P),
        ∀ x : B ⧸ P,
          e (algebraMap (B ⧸ P) (Localization.Away (Ideal.Quotient.mk P φ)) x) =
            algebraMap (B ⧸ P) (FractionRing (B ⧸ P)) x) ∧
      (@nhds (B ⧸ P)
        ((ValuationRing.valuation (B ⧸ P) (FractionRing (B ⧸ P))).comap
          (algebraMap (B ⧸ P) (FractionRing (B ⧸ P)))).subgroups_basis.topology 0).HasBasis
        (fun n : ℕ ↦ 1 ≤ n)
        (fun n : ℕ ↦ (Ideal.span {Ideal.Quotient.mk P φ ^ n} : Set (B ⧸ P))) := by
  sorry

end RingTheory
